    "q-143252": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143252",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ is a sample of size $n=100$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =35$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =35,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=35.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
